WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.040
Two parent plants can produce a hundred seeds,

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:05.160
and no two are quite the same.

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:07.840
Can you predict which genotypes their offspring could

00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:08.520
have?

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:11.040
You can, with a few pop-beads on a

00:00:11.240 --> 00:00:12.000
desk.

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:14.880
In this practical you'll model meiosis with crossing

00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:18.400
over, then fertilisation and a mutation, and use

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:20.320
the model to predict the variety in the

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:23.280
offspring, and to see where those predictions can

00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:24.000
go wrong.

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:29.600
The aim: model meiosis, fertilisation and mutation to

00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:32.320
predict the variation in offspring genotypes.

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:36.560
You need pop-beads in two colours, centromere pieces,

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:40.240
small stickers, a coin, a die, string loops

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:43.520
for cells, blank cards and a results sheet.

00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:46.320
Plum beads stand for chromosomes the parent got

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:48.640
from its mother, and teal for those from

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:49.360
its father.

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:51.920
The colours let you track where every piece

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:52.640
came from.

00:00:53.867 --> 00:00:56.666
Step one: build a parent cell with two

00:00:56.747 --> 00:00:58.506
pairs of chromosomes.

00:00:58.587 --> 00:01:00.506
Make a long plum chain and a long

00:01:00.667 --> 00:01:03.466
teal chain the same length, then a short

00:01:03.547 --> 00:01:04.866
pair the same way.

00:01:05.227 --> 00:01:08.666
Stick alleles at matching positions: purple and tall

00:01:08.747 --> 00:01:11.786
on the long plum chromosome, white and short

00:01:11.867 --> 00:01:14.306
on the long teal one, round on the

00:01:14.347 --> 00:01:16.826
short plum one, and wrinkled on the short

00:01:16.907 --> 00:01:17.546
teal one.

00:01:18.107 --> 00:01:21.706
Matching positions matter, because homologous chromosomes carry the

00:01:21.787 --> 00:01:24.026
same genes in the same places.

00:01:25.234 --> 00:01:25.953
Step two:

00:01:26.514 --> 00:01:29.793
the DNA is copied before meiosis starts.

00:01:30.114 --> 00:01:33.073
So build an identical twin of each chain,

00:01:33.154 --> 00:01:35.793
same colour, same stickers, and join the pair

00:01:35.874 --> 00:01:37.073
with a centromere piece.

00:01:37.714 --> 00:01:40.593
Each chromosome is now two sister chromatids.

00:01:41.074 --> 00:01:44.273
Count them: there are still four chromosomes, because

00:01:44.354 --> 00:01:46.913
you count centromeres, not chromatids.

00:01:48.134 --> 00:01:51.933
Step three, in prophase one: crossing over.

00:01:52.214 --> 00:01:54.933
Pair each long chromosome with its partner, and

00:01:55.054 --> 00:01:57.493
roll a die: cross over only on a

00:01:57.654 --> 00:01:58.293
six.

00:01:58.534 --> 00:02:01.733
Unclip one plum chromatid and one teal chromatid

00:02:01.814 --> 00:02:04.453
at the same point, between the two genes,

00:02:04.494 --> 00:02:06.093
and swap the end pieces.

00:02:06.294 --> 00:02:09.173
Break at exactly matching points, because a real

00:02:09.254 --> 00:02:12.293
crossover exchanges the same stretch of genes, so

00:02:12.374 --> 00:02:13.893
nothing is lost or doubled.

00:02:14.294 --> 00:02:17.253
Now one chromatid carries purple with short, and

00:02:17.454 --> 00:02:19.253
another carries white with tall.

00:02:19.654 --> 00:02:21.573
Record those new combinations.

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:26.320
Step four, in metaphase one: line the pairs

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:28.240
up across the middle of the cell.

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:30.640
Flip the coin for each pair to decide

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:33.080
which side the plum chromosome goes.

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:36.160
The coin makes each pair's direction random, and

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:38.000
independent of the other pair.

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:41.440
Pull each pair apart into two new loops.

00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:44.640
Then, for meiosis two, pull the sister chromatids

00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:46.480
apart in each loop.

00:02:46.520 --> 00:02:49.120
You end up with four gametes, each with

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:51.520
one long and one short chromosome.

00:02:52.734 --> 00:02:56.013
Step five: record each gamete's alleles on a

00:02:56.094 --> 00:02:58.893
card, then rebuild and run it again with

00:02:59.054 --> 00:03:00.413
different coin results.

00:03:00.734 --> 00:03:03.373
Without crossing over, you'll only ever find four

00:03:03.454 --> 00:03:07.213
types: purple and tall, or white and short,

00:03:07.294 --> 00:03:09.373
each with round or with wrinkled.

00:03:09.694 --> 00:03:12.333
That's two choices for each of two pairs:

00:03:12.414 --> 00:03:13.373
two times two.

00:03:14.094 --> 00:03:16.253
Crossing over adds the mixed types.

00:03:16.654 --> 00:03:19.373
They're less common because, like your die, a

00:03:19.454 --> 00:03:22.093
crossover between those two genes happens in only

00:03:22.254 --> 00:03:23.213
some cells.

00:03:24.434 --> 00:03:26.753
Step six: fertilisation.

00:03:27.234 --> 00:03:29.313
A partner group builds a second parent the

00:03:29.394 --> 00:03:32.033
same way and keeps every gamete card.

00:03:32.354 --> 00:03:34.673
Each group shuffles its cards face down and

00:03:34.794 --> 00:03:36.593
draws one at random.

00:03:36.674 --> 00:03:39.153
Put the two gametes together in one loop.

00:03:39.234 --> 00:03:41.393
That's the zygote, back to two of each

00:03:41.474 --> 00:03:42.593
chromosome.

00:03:42.794 --> 00:03:46.433
Record its genotype, return the cards, and repeat.

00:03:46.474 --> 00:03:50.353
The random draw models random fertilisation: any gamete

00:03:50.434 --> 00:03:51.793
can meet any gamete.

00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:55.000
Step seven: mutation.

00:03:55.560 --> 00:03:59.080
Before one fertilisation, swap one sticker in one

00:03:59.160 --> 00:04:01.240
gamete for a new allele in a new

00:04:01.320 --> 00:04:01.720
colour.

00:04:02.280 --> 00:04:04.280
That models a DNA change in a cell

00:04:04.360 --> 00:04:05.400
that makes gametes.

00:04:05.960 --> 00:04:08.120
The offspring from that gamete carries an allele

00:04:08.200 --> 00:04:09.640
that neither parent had.

00:04:10.280 --> 00:04:14.680
Crossing over, assortment and fertilisation only reshuffle alleles.

00:04:15.160 --> 00:04:17.160
Only mutation makes a new one.

00:04:18.367 --> 00:04:20.686
Here's an example class result for just the

00:04:20.767 --> 00:04:23.726
seed-shape gene, from sixteen fertilisations.

00:04:24.367 --> 00:04:27.566
Each parent gives round or wrinkled equally often,

00:04:27.647 --> 00:04:30.766
so four pairings are equally likely: round with

00:04:30.847 --> 00:04:34.206
round, round with wrinkled, wrinkled with round, and

00:04:34.287 --> 00:04:35.326
wrinkled with wrinkled.

00:04:35.967 --> 00:04:37.726
Two of the four give one of each.

00:04:38.207 --> 00:04:41.246
So out of sixteen, the model expects four,

00:04:41.327 --> 00:04:42.366
eight and four.

00:04:42.927 --> 00:04:45.486
The class got three, nine and four.

00:04:45.967 --> 00:04:48.766
Close, but not exact, because each draw is

00:04:49.007 --> 00:04:49.486
random.

00:04:50.700 --> 00:04:52.340
Now assess the model.

00:04:52.620 --> 00:04:54.740
It represents several things well.

00:04:55.020 --> 00:04:57.900
Matching sticker positions show that homologues carry the

00:04:57.980 --> 00:04:59.140
same genes.

00:04:59.420 --> 00:05:02.540
The swap shows crossing over making new combinations

00:05:02.620 --> 00:05:03.980
without new alleles.

00:05:04.540 --> 00:05:07.740
The coin makes each pair's direction random, and

00:05:07.820 --> 00:05:11.580
joining two gametes shows fertilisation restoring the chromosome

00:05:11.740 --> 00:05:11.980
number.

00:05:12.620 --> 00:05:15.260
And it predicts which gamete types are possible.

00:05:16.467 --> 00:05:17.986
Its limits matter too.

00:05:18.467 --> 00:05:22.226
Two pairs give only four whole-chromosome combinations, so

00:05:22.267 --> 00:05:25.186
the model hides most of the variety: humans'

00:05:25.347 --> 00:05:27.986
twenty-three pairs give over eight million.

00:05:28.547 --> 00:05:30.786
You chose where the crossover went, but in

00:05:30.867 --> 00:05:33.346
real cells its position varies, and there can

00:05:33.427 --> 00:05:34.066
be several.

00:05:34.627 --> 00:05:37.586
You chose the mutation, but real mutations are

00:05:37.627 --> 00:05:40.426
rare and random, and many have no effect.

00:05:40.707 --> 00:05:43.266
And beads with stickers show alleles, but not

00:05:43.307 --> 00:05:45.746
the DNA itself, or the spindle that moves

00:05:45.827 --> 00:05:46.866
the chromosomes.

00:05:48.067 --> 00:05:50.546
To keep it valid, so it really tests

00:05:50.627 --> 00:05:53.906
what meiosis does, follow the real rules every

00:05:53.987 --> 00:05:57.266
time: break at matching points, roll and flip

00:05:57.347 --> 00:05:59.906
fresh each run, and draw at random.

00:06:00.547 --> 00:06:03.746
To make it reliable, so repeats agree, repeat

00:06:03.907 --> 00:06:06.866
many times and pool the class results, because

00:06:06.947 --> 00:06:08.786
a few trials can land far from the

00:06:08.867 --> 00:06:10.626
prediction just by chance.

00:06:11.834 --> 00:06:12.473
Your turn.

00:06:13.034 --> 00:06:15.353
A group models two parents that each have

00:06:15.434 --> 00:06:18.393
one round and one wrinkled allele, but runs

00:06:18.474 --> 00:06:20.793
only four fertilisations.

00:06:20.874 --> 00:06:23.033
None of the four offspring has two wrinkled

00:06:23.114 --> 00:06:24.153
alleles.

00:06:24.234 --> 00:06:27.033
They conclude that offspring with two wrinkled alleles

00:06:27.154 --> 00:06:29.353
can't occur from these parents.

00:06:29.434 --> 00:06:31.433
Evaluate their conclusion.

00:06:31.594 --> 00:06:32.873
Pause here to answer.

00:06:43.300 --> 00:06:45.500
The conclusion isn't supported.

00:06:45.780 --> 00:06:48.340
Each parent can give a wrinkled allele, so

00:06:48.580 --> 00:06:51.060
two wrinkled alleles are expected about one time

00:06:51.140 --> 00:06:52.020
in four.

00:06:52.340 --> 00:06:54.420
Each offspring has a three in four chance

00:06:54.460 --> 00:06:56.980
of missing out, so all four miss out

00:06:57.140 --> 00:06:58.580
about one time in three.

00:06:59.140 --> 00:07:01.300
They should repeat many more times and pool

00:07:01.340 --> 00:07:02.340
the results.

00:07:02.660 --> 00:07:04.740
If you said the model is wrong, it

00:07:04.820 --> 00:07:07.540
isn't: the sample is just too small.

00:07:08.767 --> 00:07:12.606
So, to model offspring variation: track each parent's

00:07:12.727 --> 00:07:16.606
chromosomes by colour, copy before dividing, cross over

00:07:16.647 --> 00:07:19.806
on a six at matching points, randomise every

00:07:19.887 --> 00:07:23.326
pair and every fertilisation, add a mutation on

00:07:23.407 --> 00:07:26.006
purpose, and repeat enough times to trust the

00:07:26.047 --> 00:07:26.966
pattern.

00:07:27.487 --> 00:07:30.366
Next: Punnett squares and pedigrees, the quick way

00:07:30.447 --> 00:07:32.846
to predict crosses like these on paper.

00:07:34.067 --> 00:07:35.506
Here are the key notes.

00:07:35.747 --> 00:07:37.666
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:37.747 --> 00:07:38.546
to copy them.
